## 1. emit-x86-ssa ASM-FAILs (14 fixtures)

All 14 emit-x86-ssa outputs fail `as --64` with `operand type mismatch for movq` or `sub`. The pattern is consistent: the SSA emitter produces a 32-bit operand (imm or reg) with a 64-bit `q` suffix. This is a real bug in `x86_ssa_emit.oo` opcode handling, not a fixture bug.

List:

* aarch64_if_ovf — `movq` operand mismatch on line 9-10
* open92_bare — `sub` operand mismatch on line 7
* prove_prod_adler32 — `sub` operand mismatch on line 7
* prove_prod_canconst — same
* prove_prod_fnv1a — same
* prove_prod_g4_compilers_macho_linker — same
* prove_prod_g4_emb_mmu_token_map — same
* prove_prod_g4_sha256_engine — same
* prove_prod_hashfnv — same
* prove_prod_hcrc32 — same
* prove_prod_murmur3 — same
* prove_prod_rng — same
* prove_prod_tfuzz — same
* test_algebraic_invariants — same

Fix: `x86_ssa_emit_function` and `x86_ssa_format_inst` need to widen all immediates to 64-bit before emitting `movq`/`subq`/`addq`. Today `inst_imm_vals[i]` is `Int` (signed 64-bit) but the emitter sometimes uses the value as 32-bit. ROADMAP §5.1 row "Per-block register remap in x86_ssa_emit_function" is the right place to also fix this.

